import OddPerfect.EqualExponentSupportModFour

/-!
# Equal exponents: support certificates with sources that are 3 modulo 4 (data)

For each prime `l` in 137, 149, 157, 173, 181, 193, 197, 233, 241, 269, 281, 293, 313, 317 a
certificate accepted by `EqSupport.supportCheckFour` with target count `7` (see
`OddPerfect.EqualExponentSupportModFour`): every source other than `l` is `3` modulo `4`, so no
bound on the special prime is needed. The certificates come from a search outside Lean. Only the
kernel check below is trusted. Data module: it proves nothing about odd perfect numbers alone.

Restricted result; no published source was checked; novelty not verified.
-/

namespace OddPerfect.EqSupport

open OddPerfect.EqCount (big witnessOK)

